ted View (CPCV) was around $0.40. It's worth noting that the median CPCV is significantly lower ($0.06), suggesting a skewed distribution, possibly due to some campaigns having very low video completions or high daily spend.\\n\\n**Anomaly Detection:**\\nUsing a 3-standard-deviation rule, no significant anomalies were detected in any of the key metrics (`impressions`, `unique_reach`, `clicks`, `CTR`, `CPC`, `CPCV`, `cp_daily_spend`). This indicates that the daily performance metrics generally stayed within expected ranges during the observed period.\\n\\n**Weekly Performance Trends:**\\n\\nThe data covers two weeks (Week 1 of 2023 and Week 2 of 2023).\\n\\n**Weekly Performance Trends:**\\n\\n| Year | Week | impressions | unique_reach | clicks | CTR      | CPC      | CPCV     | cp_daily_spend |\\n| :--- | :--- | :---------- | :----------- | :----- | :------- | :------- | :------- | :------------- |\\n| 2023 | 1    | 161000      | 128600       | 3250   | 2.347959 | 0.391307 | 0.399580 | 870.0          |\\n| 2023 | 2    | 160500      | 128200       | 3250   | 2.347959 | 0.391307 | 0.399580 | 875.0          |\\n\\n*   **Consistency Across Weeks:** The weekly performance metrics for impressions, unique reach, and clicks show remarkable consistency between Week 1 and Week 2.\\n*   **CTR, CPC, CPCV Stability:** The average weekly CTR, CPC, and CPCV remained almost identical across the two weeks, suggesting stable campaign performance over this period.\\n*   **Ad Spend:** The `cp_daily_spend` sum for Week 2 ($875.0) was slightly higher than Week 1 ($870.0), indicating a minor increase in overall expenditure.\\n\\nIn conclusion, the campaign performance remained relatively stable and consistent across the two weeks analyzed, with no significant anomalies observed in the daily metrics.\"\n    }\n}"
